{"message": "Empirical evidence, clearly. If I have to pick a side, that's the one, and it's not a close call. The reason is simple: an empirical claim can be checked by someone who wants me to be wrong, and a philosophical argument mostly can't. Elegant reasoning is very good at producing internally consistent pictures of the world that turn out to describe nothing. So when the two conflict on a factual question — say, whether raising the minimum wage costs jobs, or whether a policy actually reduced crime — I'll take the messy observational study over the clean a priori argument every time.

That said, I don't think the split is as clean as the question implies. Data doesn't tell you which questions are worth asking, and it doesn't tell you what to value once you have answers. Intuition is doing real work when it says "this correlation smells like a confound" or "that's the interesting anomaly" — that's pattern recognition, not mysticism. And on genuinely normative questions, empirical evidence runs out and I'm left arguing, which is closer to philosophy.

Where I get impatient is philosophy used as a substitute for looking. A lot of confident claims about how people behave would be settled in an afternoon with a decent dataset, and instead get decades of armchair reasoning. I'd rather be corrected by reality than be consistent with myself."}
